From eb701592145f3fd18f3f169f4dcb43dbc01fa525 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Randy Northrup <randy.northrup@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 23:53:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] usb: xhci: don't drop endpoints of a device that is
 already gone

When a hub is unplugged, usb_disable_device() drops the endpoints of every
device behind it, and xhci_check_bandwidth() commits that with a Configure
Endpoint command. By then the device is already NOTATTACHED. The Disable
Slot that follows (xhci_free_dev) frees everything in the controller anyway,
and usb_disable_device() ignores the result.

On the Nintendo Switch (Tegra X1, xhci-tegra) the controller firmware never
completes that command for a device behind a vanished hub (seen with a
DisplayLink dock behind a VIA hub). After the 5 s command timer, the abort
path in xhci_handle_command_timeout() spins with interrupts off on cpu0 for
~18 s. That stalls the whole SoC and usually ends in "Watchdog detected
hard LOCKUP on cpu 0".

Skip the command for a NOTATTACHED device and just reset the software
bandwidth state.

Tested on a Switch Lite (4.9.140-l4t) with the same logic in an out-of-tree
module (a kallsyms hook of check_bandwidth): a raw dock pull while
streaming tears down all 6 devices in ~180 ms with no stall, where the
system used to hang.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/usb/host/xhci.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c b/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
index 8c46b5d3..944ef6a5 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
@@ -2779,6 +2779,19 @@ int xhci_check_bandwidth(struct usb_hcd *hcd, struct usb_device *udev)
 		(xhci->xhc_state & XHCI_STATE_REMOVING))
 		return -ENODEV;
 
+	/*
+	 * The device is already gone (its hub was unplugged). Dropping its
+	 * endpoints with a Configure Endpoint command is pointless: the
+	 * Disable Slot that follows frees everything in the controller, and
+	 * usb_disable_device() ignores our return value. Some controllers
+	 * (Tegra X1) never complete that command for a device behind a
+	 * vanished hub, so skip it.
+	 */
+	if (udev->state == USB_STATE_NOTATTACHED) {
+		xhci_reset_bandwidth(hcd, udev);
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
 	xhci_dbg(xhci, "%s called for udev %p\n", __func__, udev);
 	virt_dev = xhci->devs[udev->slot_id];
 
